# ---------------- Part 3 answers ----------------
Q = {}
Q[1] = f"""Model A (OLS on decile_score) and Model B (logistic on two_year_recid, the "is_recidivist" label) were fitted on the same {R['n_train']} training rows with identical features, yet on the {R['n_test']} test rows they disagree on {P1['mismatch']} people, a mismatch of {pc(P1['mismatch_pct'],2)}. The agreement matrix shows {cm[0][0]} low/low, {cm[1][1]} high/high, {cm[1][0]} cases that A calls high but B calls low, and only {cm[0][1]} cases the other way. The asymmetry is the whole story. A's threshold is its median linear prediction, {f(P1['median_predA'],3)} decile points, so by construction exactly {pc(P1['highA_pct'])} of the test set is "high risk". B's threshold is a probability of 0.5, but the median of B's predicted probabilities is only {f(P1['pB_median'],3)}, so p ≥ 0.5 flags just {pc(P1['highB_pct'])}. In effect B cuts at the {f(100*P1['pB_quantile_used'],1)}th percentile while A cuts at the 50th. The two rankings are almost the same (correlation between A's predictions and B's probabilities = {f(P1['corr_AB'],3)}), so the disagreement comes mainly from where each cut sits, not from different views of who is riskier. The two thresholds also live on different scales. A's median is relative: it adapts to whatever the sample looks like and has no link to the base rate. B's 0.5 is absolute and tied to the observed recidivism base rate of {pc(100*R['base_rate'])}, which is below 50%. There is a second, smaller source: the targets differ. The decile score is COMPAS's own judgement (OLS R² = {f(P1['olsR2'],3)}), and it puts much more weight on race than the outcome does. The African-American coefficient is {f(P1['coefA']['race_AfricanAmerican'],3)} decile points in A against {f(P1['coefB']['race_AfricanAmerican'],3)} log-odds in B. Against real outcomes, A's labels are {pc(100*P1['accA'])} accurate and B's are {pc(100*P1['accB'])}. So the {cm[1][0]} "A-high, B-low" people are mostly people whom the score-imitating model ranks above the median but whose actual re-arrest probability is below one half."""

r_ = lambda m, p: sx.loc[p]
Q[2] = f"""Two splits were tested. By sex, the race effect reverses sign, which is a Simpson-type pattern. In the full-data logistic model the African-American coefficient is {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full'])} (95% CI {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full_lo'])} to {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full_hi'])}, p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full_p'])}), so it is not significant. Among men (n = {R['n_male']}) it is positive at {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male'])} (CI {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male_lo'])} to {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male_hi'])}, p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male_p'])}). Among women (n = {R['n_female']}) it is negative at {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female'])} (CI {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female_lo'])} to {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female_hi'])}, p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female_p'])}). The two subgroup coefficients differ significantly (z = {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','z'],2)}, p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','p_diff'])}). Pooling hides opposite associations because men make up {pc(100*R['n_male']/R['n_filtered'])} of the data and drive the aggregate. By race (African-American n = {int(S('age','African-American').n)} vs Caucasian n = {int(S('age','Caucasian').n)}), no significant predictor flips sign; only the null juvenile-felony and misdemeanour terms do. The effects do differ in size, though. Being male carries log-odds of {f(S('sex_Male','African-American').coef)} (CI {f(S('sex_Male','African-American').ci_lo)} to {f(S('sex_Male','African-American').ci_hi)}, p {pv(S('sex_Male','African-American').p)}) for African-American defendants but only {f(S('sex_Male','Caucasian').coef)} (CI {f(S('sex_Male','Caucasian').ci_lo)} to {f(S('sex_Male','Caucasian').ci_hi)}, p = {pv(S('sex_Male','Caucasian').p)}) for Caucasians (difference p = {pv(sdiff.loc['sex_Male','p'])}). Priors per SD are {f(S('priors_count','African-American').coef)} vs {f(S('priors_count','Caucasian').coef)} (difference p = {pv(sdiff.loc['priors_count','p'])}), and "other" juvenile offences are {f(S('juv_other_count','African-American').coef)} vs {f(S('juv_other_count','Caucasian').coef)} (difference p = {pv(sdiff.loc['juv_other_count','p'])}). Policy implication: one pooled coefficient vector is a misleading description of every group. A tool that says "race adds nothing" (pooled p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full_p'])}) is quietly averaging an upward shift for men with a downward one for women. Auditors should require models to be validated by subgroup, including intersections such as sex × race, before deployment. Where effects truly differ, the agency should publish group-specific calibration rather than one score, and it should not read a non-significant pooled race term as evidence that the tool is race-neutral."""

rows4 = ["age", "sex_Male", "race_AfricanAmerican", "charge_Felony", "juv_other_count"]
tbl3 = "; ".join(f"{p}: {f(ovb.loc[p,'full'])} → {f(ovb.loc[p,'reduced'])} ({ovb.loc[p,'pct_chg']:+.1f}%)" for p in rows4)
Q[3] = f"""I dropped priors_count from Model B. It is the strongest predictor in the full model (log-odds {f(R['ovb']['priors_coef'])} per SD), and it is correlated with race (r = {f(R['ovb']['corr_with_priors']['race_AfricanAmerican'])} with the African-American dummy), sex (r = {f(R['ovb']['corr_with_priors']['sex_Male'])}), felony charge (r = {f(R['ovb']['corr_with_priors']['charge_Felony'])}) and juvenile records (r = {f(R['ovb']['corr_with_priors']['juv_misd_count'])} for misdemeanours). That makes it a textbook confounder. Coefficients change from full to reduced as follows (% change relative to |full|; the full table is in Part 2.2): {tbl3}. The biggest shift is {big}: it rises from a null {f(ovb.loc[big,'full'])} (p = {pv(ovb.loc[big,'full_p'])}) to {f(ovb.loc[big,'reduced'])} (p = {pv(ovb.loc[big,'reduced_p'])}), a {ovb.loc[big,'pct_chg']:+.0f}% change. The percentage looks extreme because the base is close to zero. The substantive point is that a race effect appears from nothing once criminal history is omitted. The juvenile felony and misdemeanour coefficients behave the same way ({ovb.loc['juv_fel_count','pct_chg']:+.0f}% and {ovb.loc['juv_misd_count','pct_chg']:+.0f}%), becoming significant proxies for priors. Age weakens by {f(abs(ovb.loc['age','pct_chg']),1)}% because younger people have fewer priors, so age was partly suppressing the priors signal. Model fit falls (AIC {f(R['ovb']['aic_full'],1)} → {f(R['ovb']['aic_red'],1)}). The direction matches the omitted-variable-bias formula: bias = β_priors × the regression of priors on each variable. β_priors is positive and priors correlate positively with being African-American, so the race coefficient is pushed upward. The lesson for policy is that whether a model "uses race" depends on what else it includes. Removing a legitimate covariate can make a protected attribute look predictive, and keeping it can hide the fact that the covariate itself carries the effects of policing that differ by race."""

Q[4] = f"""A concrete method is a negative-control and proxy audit, run at the start of any deployment. Step 1: list plausible unmeasured confounders for this setting, such as policing intensity in the defendant's neighbourhood, poverty, and access to a lawyer. Step 2: link available proxies to the records, for example census-tract arrest rates per capita or public-defender status taken from court dockets. Step 3: refit the model with and without each proxy and track the coefficients of the protected attributes, as in Part 2.2. There, adding or removing a single covariate moved the African-American coefficient from {f(ovb.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full'])} to {f(ovb.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','reduced'])}, so movements of that size are detectable. Step 4: add a negative-control outcome, meaning something the features should not cause but the confounder would, such as being arrested for an offence later dismissed for lack of evidence. If "race" or "priors" predict dismissed arrests as strongly as convictions, the signal is coming from enforcement, not behaviour. Step 5: run a sensitivity analysis (Rosenbaum bounds or E-values) to ask how strong an unseen confounder would have to be to explain away the race association. Step 6: check heterogeneity. Part 2.1 showed that the pooled race coefficient {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full'])} hides {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male'])} for men and {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female'])} for women. Unexpected sign changes across strata are a warning sign that something omitted varies by group. In Broward County this could be done with Florida court data: compute re-arrest rates per officer-hour by district, add them as a covariate, and see whether the African-American priors gradient ({f(S('priors_count','African-American').coef)} vs {f(S('priors_count','Caucasian').coef)} per SD in the subgroup models) shrinks. If it does, part of what "priors" measures is exposure to policing, a hidden confound that a recidivism tool would otherwise turn into an individual risk label."""

Q[5] = f"""The adversarial subset holds every test row where Model B is confidently wrong: {A['count']} people, or {pc(A['pct'],2)} of the {R['n_test']} test rows. Of these, {A['n_hi']} had p ≥ 0.75 but did not reoffend, and {A['n_lo']} had p ≤ 0.25 but did. A sample of 50 drawn with seed 192 ({TR['sample_hi_n']} false-high and {TR['sample_lo_n']} false-low) differs sharply from the test set. The race mix is similar ({pc(D['Sample of 50']['% African-American'])} vs {pc(D['Full test']['% African-American'])} African-American) and so is the share of men ({pc(D['Sample of 50']['% Male'])} vs {pc(D['Full test']['% Male'])}). Age is not. Mean age is {f(D['Sample of 50']['mean age'],1)} vs {f(D['Full test']['mean age'],1)}, and {pc(D['Sample of 50']['% age>45'])} are older than 45 compared with {pc(D['Full test']['% age>45'])} in the test set. Only {pc(D['Sample of 50']['% age<25'])} are under 25 (test set {pc(D['Full test']['% age<25'])}). The shared trait is an extreme position on the model's two dominant axes. {pc(TR['pct_extreme_profile'],0)} of the 50 are either confidently-high people with 5+ priors or under 25, or confidently-low people aged 35+ with at most one prior. Within the groups, {pc(TR['hi_pct_priors_ge5'])} of the false-highs have 5+ priors, and {pc(TR['lo_pct_age_ge35'],0)} of the false-lows are aged 35+ ({pc(TR['lo_pct_age_gt45'])} over 45). The mechanism follows from the coefficients. Log-odds are linear in standardised priors ({f(P1['coefB']['priors_count'])}/SD) and age ({f(P1['coefB']['age'])}/SD). Priors have SD {f(R['priors_sd'],2)} (mean {f(R['priors_mean'],2)}), so someone with 15 priors sits {f((15-R['priors_mean'])/R['priors_sd'],2)} SD out and gains {f(P1['coefB']['priors_count']*(15-R['priors_mean'])/R['priors_sd'],2)} log-odds from priors alone, which pushes p above 0.75 whatever else is true. Older age pushes p below 0.25 in the same way. The model has no features for desistance (people who stop offending), employment or supervision, so it cannot tell which heavy-history or older people differ from the average. Its extreme probabilities are extrapolations along one direction, and the 8 features carry no information to correct them."""

Q[6] = f"""Consider two 23-year-old men facing the same felony charge, each with one prior. One is African-American, the other Caucasian. Under Model B their probabilities are almost identical, because the race coefficient is small ({f(P1['coefB']['race_AfricanAmerican'])} log-odds). Now suppose a court picks a threshold based on group statistics. At the default t = 0.5, the test set flags {pc(100*B5['sel_A'])} of African-American defendants and {pc(100*B5['sel_B'])} of Caucasians (DP = {f(B5['DP'],2)}). Among people who did not reoffend, {pc(100*B5['FPR_A'])} of African-Americans are wrongly flagged versus {pc(100*B5['FPR_B'])} of Caucasians. A reform committee might then propose group-specific cut-offs to equalise false positives, with roughly t = 0.65 for African-Americans (FPR {pc(100*sw.loc[6,'FPR_A'])}) and t = 0.45 for Caucasians (FPR {pc(100*sw.loc[4,'FPR_B'])}). Under that rule, if both men score p = 0.55, the African-American man is released and the Caucasian man is detained. Same record, same probability, opposite decision, purely because of group membership. That is disparate treatment, and it can be illegal. Under a single threshold, the African-American man faces the base-rate-driven disparity instead. His group's observed re-arrest rate in the test set is {pc(100*R['base_A'])} against {pc(100*R['base_B'])} for Caucasians, so more members of his group sit near the cut, and his group's FPR at t = 0.5 is {f(B5['FPR_A']/B5['FPR_B'],2)}× higher. Neither rule treats both men "fairly" in every sense. Group thresholds equalise error rates but break individual consistency; a single threshold keeps consistency but produces unequal error burdens. The concrete harm also differs. A false positive means pretrial detention, with possible job loss and pressure to plead guilty. A false negative is a missed re-arrest, whose cost falls on the public. Any choice should be made explicitly and recorded in writing, not left buried in a default."""

Q[7] = f"""I recommend a single threshold of t = 0.5, the same for everyone. Evidence: (1) it gives the highest test accuracy of all thresholds evaluated, {pc(100*B5['acc'])}, against {pc(100*sw.loc[4,'acc'])} at 0.45, {pc(100*sw.loc[5,'acc'])} at 0.55 and {pc(100*sw.loc[2,'acc'])} at 0.25. (2) Precision is reasonably high in both groups ({pc(100*B5['prec_A'])} for African-American and {pc(100*B5['prec_B'])} for Caucasian defendants), so most people flagged as high risk do in fact reoffend. The PP ratio is {f(B5['PP'],2)}, compared with {f(sw.loc[1,'PP'],2)} at t = 0.15. (3) A logistic model's 0.5 cut-off has a plain meaning, "more likely than not", which can be explained in court and challenged on appeal. A tuned threshold such as 0.25 also flags {pc(100*sw.loc[2,'sel_B'])} of Caucasians and {pc(100*sw.loc[2,'sel_A'])} of African-Americans, which amounts to detaining almost everyone. (4) Lowering the threshold to move DP towards parity costs accuracy: going to 0.25 reduces accuracy by {pc(t25['W_acc'])}. The strongest counter-argument is about error-rate fairness. At t = 0.5 the false-positive rate for African-American defendants who never reoffend is {pc(100*B5['FPR_A'])}, against {pc(100*B5['FPR_B'])} for Caucasians, a ratio of {f(B5['FPR_A']/B5['FPR_B'],2)}. DP is {f(B5['DP'],2)}: African-Americans are flagged at {f(B5['DP'],1)}× the Caucasian rate. Because the cost of a false positive is liberty, a critic can reasonably argue that accuracy which falls unequally on one group is not a neutral virtue. The {f(100*(B5['acc']-sw.loc[3,'acc']),1)}-point accuracy edge over t = 0.35 (DP {f(sw.loc[3,'DP'],2)}) is being paid for disproportionately by Black defendants. That critic would also note that the base-rate gap ({pc(100*R['base_A'])} vs {pc(100*R['base_B'])}) partly reflects differences in policing, so "calibrated" may itself encode bias. My defence holds only if t = 0.5 is paired with human review of every flagged case and regular monitoring of the FPR ratio."""

Q[8] = f"""In pretrial or parole decisions, I prefer equalised odds (EO) to demographic parity (DP). DP asks for equal flag rates whatever the true outcomes; EO asks that people who will not reoffend, and people who will, face equal error rates regardless of race. Because the harm of a false positive is detention, the FPR component of EO maps directly onto the injustice that matters. At t = 0.5 the test set gives DP = {f(B5['DP'],2)} and EO = {f(B5['EO'],2)} (TPR ratio {f(B5['TPR_A']/B5['TPR_B'],2)}, FPR ratio {f(B5['FPR_A']/B5['FPR_B'],2)}), so both are far from parity. Measuring fairness as closeness to 1, min(r, 1/r): moving to t = 0.25 improves DP from {f(t25['DP_from'],2)} to {f(t25['DP_to'],2)} ({pc(t25['Z'])} gain), and EO goes from {f(t25['EO_from'],2)} to {f(t25['EO_to'],2)}, a W = {pc(t25['W'])} "loss" (negative, so in fact a gain), giving cost = W/Z = {f(t25['cost'],2)}. Going to t = 0.15 gives DP {f(t15['DP_to'],3)} ({pc(t15['Z'])} gain) and EO {f(t15['EO_to'],3)} (W = {pc(t15['W'])}, cost {f(t15['cost'],2)}). So in this data a single threshold never trades DP against EO. Both approach 1 only because the threshold falls so low that almost everyone is flagged. What is actually sacrificed is accuracy and predictive parity. Accuracy falls from {pc(100*t25['acc_from'])} to {pc(100*t25['acc_to'])} at 0.25 (a {pc(t25['W_acc'])} loss, cost {f(t25['cost_acc'],2)} per unit of DP gain) and to {pc(100*t15['acc_to'])} at 0.15. Predictive parity worsens from {f(t15['PP_from'],2)} to {f(t15['PP_to'],2)} at 0.15 ({pc(t15['W_pp'])} loss). The Caucasian precision falls to {f(sw.loc[1,'prec_B'],2)}, so a "high-risk" label means something different for each group. Fairness achieved by flagging everyone is meaningless: liberty is sacrificed equally rather than allocated fairly. With base rates of {pc(100*R['base_A'])} vs {pc(100*R['base_B'])}, the impossibility result (Kleinberg, Chouldechova) applies: calibration and equal error rates cannot both hold. Real EO therefore needs group-aware post-processing or better features, not a moved global threshold."""

P4 = {}
P4["Direct answer"] = f"""No. A single accuracy figure, such as this model's {pc(100*P1['accB'])} at t = 0.5, cannot tell us whether a recidivism tool is fit to use on people, because it hides who bears the errors, how the threshold was chosen, and what the label actually measures."""
P4["Why a single number fails"] = f"""First, aggregate accuracy averages over groups whose error profiles are very different. At the same t = 0.5, {pc(100*B5['FPR_A'])} of African-American defendants who never reoffended were flagged high risk, compared with {pc(100*B5['FPR_B'])} of Caucasians. Accuracy within the two groups barely differs ({pc(100*B5['acc_A'])} vs {pc(100*B5['acc_B'])} at t = 0.5), so a headline number cannot reveal a near-doubling of wrongful flags. The Simpson analysis makes the same point about coefficients. The pooled race effect ({f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full'])}, p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full_p'])}) looks negligible, yet among men it is {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male'])} and among women {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female'])}, and both are significant. One summary averaged two opposite truths.

Second, any number depends on a threshold that someone chose. Model A and Model B rank people almost identically (correlation {f(P1['corr_AB'],3)}), yet they disagree on {pc(P1['mismatch_pct'],2)} of cases only because A cuts at its median and B at 0.5. Across the sweep, accuracy ranges from {pc(100*sw.acc.min())} to {pc(100*sw.acc.max())} and DP from {f(sw.DP.min(),2)} to {f(sw.DP.max(),2)} for the same fitted model. Reporting one point on that curve hides a policy decision inside a technical default.

Third, the metric depends on how the model is specified and on what the label means. Dropping one confounder, priors_count, moved the African-American coefficient from {f(ovb.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full'])} to {f(ovb.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','reduced'])}. Whether the model "uses race" therefore depends on which other variables are included. The label two_year_recid records re-arrest, not reoffending, so policing intensity enters both the label and the strongest feature, prior arrests. A model can be very accurate at predicting arrests and still be unfair.

Fourth, averages hide confident failures. {A['count']} test cases ({pc(A['pct'],2)}) were wrong at probabilities beyond 0.75 or 0.25, and they cluster among older people and people with long records ({pc(D['Sample of 50']['% age>45'])} of the sampled 50 were over 45, against {pc(D['Full test']['% age>45'])} in the test set). For them, the model's confidence is the problem, and accuracy does not measure confidence. A judge who sees "p = 0.80" reads it as near-certainty, but for a long-record defendant in this region of the data it is an extrapolation along a single coefficient, not evidence about that individual."""
P4["Grounding in numbers"] = f"""Concretely, the {pc(100*P1['accB'])} accuracy sits alongside DP = {f(B5['DP'],2)}, EO = {f(B5['EO'],2)} and PP = {f(B5['PP'],2)}. Pushing DP to {f(t15['DP_to'],2)} would require t = 0.15, where accuracy falls to {pc(100*t15['acc_to'])}. Base rates differ ({pc(100*R['base_A'])} vs {pc(100*R['base_B'])}), so no single threshold can satisfy all criteria at once. Meanwhile {A['count']} people were confidently misjudged, and removing priors_count changed the race coefficient by {ovb.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','pct_chg']:+.0f}%."""
P4["Closing"] = """A responsible audit reports a dashboard, not a score. It should cover error rates by group and by intersection, the full threshold curve, how coefficients hold up when variables are added or removed, and the confident failures, together with a plain statement of which fairness criterion was chosen and who pays for that choice in liberty."""

