def fig(p, cap, w="88%"): return f'<img src="figures/{p}" style="width:{w}"><div class="fig">{cap}</div>'
H = f"""<html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><style>{css}</style></head><body>
<h1>CSET485 AI and Society – Assignment 1</h1>
<p><b>Roll number:</b> S24CSEU1192 &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>Dataset:</b> ProPublica COMPAS <code>compas-scores-two-years.csv</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <b>random_state:</b> 192, 70/30 train/test split.<br>
All numbers are produced by <code>analysis.ipynb</code> (executed with nbconvert) and read from <code>results.json</code>; nothing is typed by hand.</p>

<h2>1. Preprocessing</h2>
<p>The raw file has {R['n_raw']} rows × {R['n_cols_raw']} columns. <b>Missing values:</b> the columns with missing values are listed below. Most are violent-recidivism or re-offence fields that are empty by design when no event happened. <code>days_b_screening_arrest</code> is missing for {R['missing_raw']['days_b_screening_arrest']} rows, which the filter drops. After filtering, the modelling columns have {R['missing_after']} missing values, so no imputation was needed.</p>
{table(miss,0)}
<p><b>ProPublica filters</b> (justification): ±30 days between arrest and screening, so that the COMPAS score belongs to the charge we observe; <code>is_recid ≠ −1</code>, because −1 means no case record and an unknown outcome; <code>c_charge_degree ≠ 'O'</code>, because ordinary traffic offences carry no jail time; <code>score_text ≠ 'N/A'</code>, because these rows have no valid score. This two-year file was already partly cleaned by ProPublica, so in practice only the first filter removes rows: {R['n_raw']} → {R['n_filtered']}.</p>
{table(filt,0)}
<p><b>Encoding:</b> one-hot with reference categories: sex (Female), race (Caucasian; Asian and Native American merged into Other), charge degree (Misdemeanour). Race counts: {', '.join(f'{k} {v}' for k,v in R['race_counts'].items())}. <b>Scaling:</b> the numeric features (age, priors_count, juv_fel/misd/other counts) are standardised with a StandardScaler fitted on the training set only. <b>Outliers</b> (above Q3 + 1.5·IQR): {', '.join(f'{k} {v}' for k,v in R['outliers_iqr'].items())}. They are kept, because these are real criminal histories, the skew is in the right tail, and removing them would exclude exactly the people the tool is aimed at. <b>Features</b> (identical for both models, no score columns): age, priors_count, juv_fel_count, juv_misd_count, juv_other_count, sex_Male, race dummies, charge_Felony. Split: {R['n_train']} training rows / {R['n_test']} test rows. Two-year recidivism base rate: {pc(100*R['base_rate'])}.</p>

<h2>2. Part 1 – Model A (OLS) vs Model B (logistic)</h2>
<p>Model A: OLS predicting <code>decile_score</code> (R² = {f(P1['olsR2'])}). Model B: logistic regression predicting <code>two_year_recid</code>, the <b>"is_recidivist"</b> outcome (1 = re-arrested within two years). On the test set, A is thresholded at its median prediction <b>{f(P1['median_predA'])}</b> (≥ median = high risk) and B at p ≥ 0.5. The median of B's probabilities is {f(P1['pB_median'])}.</p>
{table(cmdf,0)}
<div class="box"><b>Mismatch:</b> {P1['mismatch']} of {R['n_test']} = <b>{pc(P1['mismatch_pct'],2)}</b>. A flags {pc(P1['highA_pct'])} as high risk and B flags {pc(P1['highB_pct'])}. Correlation between A's predictions and B's probabilities: {f(P1['corr_AB'])}. Accuracy against two_year_recid: A {pc(100*P1['accA'])}, B {pc(100*P1['accB'])}.</div>
<p><b>Why they differ:</b> the ranking is almost the same, but the cuts are not. A's median is a relative cut, so it labels 50% as high risk by construction. B's 0.5 is an absolute probability, and because the base rate is below 50% it labels fewer people. In addition, A learns COMPAS's scoring, which puts much more weight on race ({f(P1['coefA']['race_AfricanAmerican'])} decile points) than the real outcome does in B ({f(P1['coefB']['race_AfricanAmerican'])} log-odds).</p>
{table(coefs)}

<h2>3. Part 2 – Statistical analyses</h2>
<h3>2.1 Simpson's paradox</h3>
<p>Logistic models for two_year_recid were fitted on the full filtered data (n = {R['n_filtered']}) and within subgroups. Cells show coefficient [95% CI] and p-value; numeric features are per 1 SD. Split 1 compares African-American (subgroup A) with Caucasian (subgroup B) defendants:</p>
{table(srt)}
<p>Split 2 compares male (subgroup A) with female (subgroup B) defendants:</p>
{table(sxt)}
<div class="box"><b>Finding:</b> the race coefficient <b>reverses sign</b> across sex. It is {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full'])} pooled (p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','full_p'])}), +{f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male'])} among men (p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','male_p'])}) and {f(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female'])} among women (p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','female_p'])}); the difference test gives p = {pv(sx.loc['race_AfricanAmerican','p_diff'])}. In the race split no significant predictor reverses sign, but the effects differ significantly: sex_Male {f(S('sex_Male','African-American').coef)} vs {f(S('sex_Male','Caucasian').coef)} (p = {pv(sdiff.loc['sex_Male','p'])}), juv_other_count (p = {pv(sdiff.loc['juv_other_count','p'])}), priors (p = {pv(sdiff.loc['priors_count','p'])}) and age (p = {pv(sdiff.loc['age','p'])}). <b>Policy implication:</b> a pooled model averages away opposite effects in subgroups, so validation by subgroup and by intersection (e.g. sex × race) should be required before any claim that the tool is "race-neutral".</div>
{fig('fig_simpson.png','Figure 1. Coefficients with 95% CIs: full data vs African-American vs Caucasian.','80%')}

<h3>2.2 Omitted-variable bias (dropping priors_count)</h3>
{table(ovbt)}
<div class="box">The <b>biggest shift</b> is in <b>{big}</b>: {f(ovb.loc[big,'full'])} → {f(ovb.loc[big,'reduced'])} ({ovb.loc[big,'pct_chg']:+.1f}%), going from not significant to p = {pv(ovb.loc[big,'reduced_p'])}. The juvenile counts also become significant. Age weakens by {f(abs(ovb.loc['age','pct_chg']),1)}%, and charge_Felony rises by {f(ovb.loc['charge_Felony','pct_chg'],1)}%. AIC goes from {f(R['ovb']['aic_full'],1)} to {f(R['ovb']['aic_red'],1)}. Percentage changes are inflated where the full coefficient is close to 0, so the absolute changes are shown too.</div>
{fig('fig_ovb.png','Figure 2. % change in each coefficient after omitting priors_count.','72%')}

<h3>2.3 Adversarial (confidently wrong) subset</h3>
<p>Rows with p ≥ 0.75 and y = 0, or p ≤ 0.25 and y = 1: <b>{A['count']}</b> ({pc(A['pct'],2)} of the test set). {A['n_hi']} are false-high and {A['n_lo']} are false-low. A sample of 50 was drawn with seed 192 ({TR['sample_hi_n']} false-high, {TR['sample_lo_n']} false-low).</p>
{table(demo,2)}
<div class="box"><b>Shared trait:</b> the subset is older and sits at the extremes of the age and priors axes. {pc(D['Sample of 50']['% age>45'])} are over 45, against {pc(D['Full test']['% age>45'])} in the test set, and {pc(TR['pct_extreme_profile'],0)} of the 50 have an extreme profile: confidently-high cases with 5+ priors or under 25, or confidently-low cases aged 35+ with at most one prior. {pc(TR['hi_pct_priors_ge5'])} of the false-highs have 5+ priors, and {pc(TR['lo_pct_age_ge35'],0)} of the false-lows are aged 35+. <b>Why the model fails:</b> the log-odds are linear in priors ({f(P1['coefB']['priors_count'])}/SD) and age ({f(P1['coefB']['age'])}/SD), so extreme values push p to the tails, and the 8 features carry no information about desistance or context that could pull it back.</div>
{fig('fig_adversarial.png','Figure 3. Priors distribution and age vs predicted probability for the adversarial sample.','90%')}

<h3>2.4 Fairness threshold sweep (A = African-American n={R['n_test_AA']}, B = Caucasian n={R['n_test_CA']}, test set)</h3>
{table(swt)}
{fig('fig_dp_eo.png','Figure 4. DP (x) vs EO (y). Left: required 0–1.5 axes, points labelled by threshold, with off-axis thresholds listed in the box. Right: full range.','100%')}
{fig('fig_rates.png','Figure 5. TPR, FPR by group and overall accuracy across thresholds.','75%')}
<p><b>Trade-off</b> (baseline t = 0.5: DP {f(B5['DP'])}, EO {f(B5['EO'])}, PP {f(B5['PP'])}, accuracy {pc(100*B5['acc'])}). Fairness is measured as closeness to parity, min(r, 1/r). Z = % gain in DP closeness and W = % loss in EO closeness (a negative W means EO also improves).</p>
{table(tot)}
<div class="box">Improve DP from {f(t25['DP_from'],2)} to {f(t25['DP_to'],2)} ({pc(t25['Z'])} gain) and EO goes from {f(t25['EO_from'],2)} to {f(t25['EO_to'],2)} (W = {pc(t25['W'])}, so it is not a loss), giving cost = W/Z = {f(t25['cost'],2)}. For t = 0.15: DP {f(t15['DP_from'],2)} → {f(t15['DP_to'],2)} ({pc(t15['Z'])} gain), EO {f(t15['EO_from'],2)} → {f(t15['EO_to'],2)} (W = {pc(t15['W'])}), cost = {f(t15['cost'],2)}. With a single global threshold, DP and EO move <i>together</i>. The real costs are <b>accuracy</b> ({pc(t25['W_acc'])} loss at 0.25, cost {f(t25['cost_acc'],2)}) and <b>predictive parity</b> (PP {f(t15['PP_from'],2)} → {f(t15['PP_to'],2)}, {pc(t15['W_pp'])} loss at 0.15).</div>

<h2 class="pb">4. Part 3 – Discussion questions</h2>
""" + "".join(f'<div class="q"><h3>Q{i}. {QT[i]}</h3><p>{Q[i]}</p><p class="wc">[{wc(Q[i])} words]</p></div>' for i in range(1, 9)) + f"""
<h2 class="pb">5. Part 4 – Reflection: can one number certify a fair risk tool?</h2>
""" + "".join(f"<h3>{k}</h3>" + "".join(f"<p>{p}</p>" for p in v.split("\n\n")) for k, v in P4.items()) + f"""
<p class="wc">[Part 4 total: {sum(wc(v) for v in P4.values())} words; sections: {', '.join(f'{k} {wc(v)}' for k,v in P4.items())}]</p>
</body></html>"""
open("report.html", "w").write(H)
HTML("report.html", base_url=".").write_pdf("report.pdf")
json.dump({"part3": {f"Q{i}": wc(Q[i]) for i in Q}, "part4": {k: wc(v) for k, v in P4.items()}, "part4_total": sum(wc(v) for v in P4.values())}, open("wordcounts.json", "w"), indent=1)
print(open("wordcounts.json").read())
